Z||000009||DRH||06-01-2001||03:27 PM||||A lot of bigger high volume yards don't keep anything over a certain age, usually around 10 years. It's an economic decision based on how much they can get now vs. how much they MIGHT get later. Many also have a limited amount of space. They will fill that space with newer models that will make them more money because of the volume and price of parts sold.<P>I find it hard to believe GM would care enough about Fieros to pay money to get rid of one that is already junked... A special Fiero only scrap yard... where is this place? Area 51?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I remember seeing 1st generation Mustangs in pretty good shape getting crushed in the late 70s. They made like a million of those things. Why would they be relatively rare now unless a LOT of them were crushed?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||06-01-2001||03:37 PM||||Im looking for the money angle to this, from GMs perspective.<P>It wouldnt make sense for them to pay $50 or $100 per car to have them crushed if they are trying to control the used parts market, to force Fiero owners to buy new parts from them  - body panels and such - and they would have to insist the car not be stripped before its crushed.<P>More likely, maybe Gm has been hit with a bunch of lawsuits from fiero accident victims, maybe more than average, and they have been quietly settling them out of court for millions a piece?<P> that would be more of an incentive to get the spare parts off the streets, make it harder to keep the fieros on the road if you cant buy used drive trains and major components.<P>  then if they buy 10,000 crushed titles for $100 each, and that prevents one $ million lawsuit, they break even.<P>  that makes sense in a way.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Alex4mula||06-01-2001||03:58 PM||||I think maybe is to prevent having Fieros out there that may get recall work. If you find one without it you can get it to be done. I heard those are for life. So If I was GM I would pay to crush Fieros with VINs without recalls.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fiero_24||06-01-2001||05:34 PM||||I have never heard of this on a Fiero, but I have heard of it done to other cars int the past to make parts harder and more expensive to find.  I called a few scrap yards near me, and they told me that they do not do that, but have heard the rumor for certain cars.  I doubt GM would keep the Fiero off the road, just think, GM has other cars on the road that are just pure basket cases.  I think the Fiero is not a threat to them unless they plan to bring it back (fat chance) and want to force people to buy the newer model.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Toddster||06-01-2001||06:03 PM||||I'm reminded of a quote by Amon Gothe (played by Ralph Fienne -sp-) from the movie Schindler's List, "As of today, those 600 years are a rumor, they never happened, Today is history." upon announcing the "Final Solution" to his troopes.<P>Sounds like GM is trying to rewrite history too. Destroy all the Fieros and no one will ever know it existed... NOT!!!!!||||reg||1
